  "intro": "A series $\\sum a_n$ adds the terms of a sequence. A geometric series $\\sum ar^{n}$ converges iff $|r|<1$, with sum $\\frac{\\text{first term}}{1-r}$. The $n$th-term test: if $a_n\\not\\to 0$, the series diverges. The p-series $\\sum\\frac{1}{n^p}$ converges iff $p>1$. Other tools: the integral test, comparison and limit-comparison tests, and the ratio test $L=\\lim\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right|$ (converges if $L<1$, diverges if $L>1$).",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Find the sum of $\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^n$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Geometric with first term $1$ (at $n=0$) and ratio $r=\\frac{1}{3}$, and $|r|<1$.",
          "Sum $=\\frac{\\text{first term}}{1-r}=\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{1}{3}}$.",
          "Simplify: $\\frac{1}{2/3}=\\frac{3}{2}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Does $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}$ converge?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "This is a p-series with $p=2$.",
          "Since $p=2>1$, the p-series converges.",
          "(Its sum is $\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$, but convergence is what the test decides.)"
        ],
        "answer": "Converges ($p=2>1$)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Use the $n$th-term test on $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{n}{n+1}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Check $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n$: $\\frac{n}{n+1}\\to 1\\ne 0$.",
          "Because the terms do not approach $0$, the series diverges."
        ],
        "answer": "Diverges (terms $\\to 1\\ne 0$)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
